 "answer_to_user": "Under the Uranium & Nuclear Fuel Cycle megatrend, here's the cross-market player set (all surfaced on the US tape, though many are Canada-domiciled):\n\nUranium MINERS (core): Cameco (CCJ.US, ~$47bn, the big producer), NexGen Energy (NXE.US, ~$7.4bn, leader/developer), Denison Mines (DNN.US, ~$3.4bn), Energy Fuels (UUUU.US, ~$4bn), Uranium Energy Corp (UEC.US, ~$6.5bn), enCore Energy (EU.US), Ur-Energy (URG.US), plus smaller developers Eagle Nuclear (NUCL.US), Anfield (AEC.US) and Jaguar Uranium (JAGU.US). Uranium Royalty Corp (UROY.US) is the royalty/streaming beneficiary. BHP (BHP.US) shows up as a secondary/diversified-miner exposure.\n\nCONVERSION / ENRICHMENT & FUEL: Centrus Energy (LEU.US, ~$3.9bn, the US enrichment name) and ASP Isotopes (ASPI.US). FUEL FABRICATION & TECH: BWX Technologies (BWXT.US, ~$14bn) and Lightbridge (LTBR.US).\n\nSo the nuclear-revival trade spans three legs — mining (Cameco/NexGen/Denison...), enrichment (Centrus), and fuel fabrication (BWXT). Note: Kazatomprom did not appear in coverage; the miner leaders here are the North-American-listed cohort."
